claimed: json-escape-alloc -- the mirror of json-projection-alloc, on
the road that is actually hot.

`Json.escape` is `s.flatMap { ... case c => c.toString }`: a String
allocated per CHARACTER. Measured over 200k strings: 13.5 ms against
4.7 ms for an allocation-free version answering the same string, 2.9x.

Why this one and not the lossless road: escape is called by Json.print,
by Staged.scala (the compile-time encoder) and by RuntimeStaged.scala
(the run-time one). Every string through the staged doors this repo
measured at 385 ns pays it -- okay-http responses, okay-agent,
okay-mcp, every writeJson.

The care this needs: escape must keep escaping EXACTLY five characters.
This project deliberately does NOT escape \b, \f or controls -- the
unquote comment says so and calls it its own choice -- and Json.scala
says escape and unescape "must agree with this one exactly, not just
resemble it". So the test is a round trip through unquote over a
corpus that includes each of the five, the ones deliberately not
escaped, and a string with none.

Files: okay-codec Json.scala, tests, specs/codecs.md, boards.
